1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,032
The school Week Three stations require peripheral IV and tibial IO
practice. Learn them as clinical decisions followed by technical
steps, not a race to place a catheter in every patient.

2
00:00:13,032 --> 00:00:26,880
This lecture teaches the whole sequence and the reasons behind it. The
exact school catheter, drill, checkoff form and original PCP One
Ten/One Twenty slide bodies remain source gaps.

3
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:37,248
Device examples are clearly identified as manufacturer examples. They
do not prove which product the school uses or replace supervised
hands-on feedback.

4
00:00:37,248 --> 00:00:48,024
Keep necessary airway, breathing and circulation support continuous.
An invasive access attempt must not delay more important treatment or
conveyance.

5
00:00:48,024 --> 00:01:00,792
Start by stating what the route will deliver or enable in the current
plan. A technically easy vein is not an indication, and a person being
conveyed to hospital does not automatically need a field IV.

6
00:01:00,792 --> 00:01:13,968
Consider whether an appropriate less invasive route can meet the
treatment need. The choice depends on the actual patient, medicine and
authorized pathway, not a general belief that IV is always better.

7
00:01:13,968 --> 00:01:26,064
A specific pathway or an anticipated treatment by an intercepting
advanced clinician may justify access. State the real reason instead
of using keep-vein-open as a default explanation.

8
00:01:26,064 --> 00:01:36,384
Balance benefit with pain, contamination, bleeding, infiltration and
delay. The access decision is revisited if the treatment plan changes.

9
00:01:36,384 --> 00:01:45,168
The assigned PR twenty-six uses JAY to examine the access decision.
Justifiable asks whether the intended care actually needs it.

10
00:01:45,168 --> 00:01:56,856
Accountable asks whether the decision is defensible and whether you
accept responsibility for consequences. You asks you to consider the
patient's experience of benefit, burden and dignity.

11
00:01:56,856 --> 00:02:08,736
Consent to an unnecessary stick does not by itself make it appropriate
clinical care. Skill maintenance belongs on simulation equipment and
through the proper educational process.

12
00:02:08,736 --> 00:02:19,176
In a station, say the indication and a reason you would stop. That
makes your decision observable rather than hiding it behind a
successful needle placement.

13
00:02:19,176 --> 00:02:30,864
The school teaches procedures that may have additional licence,
endorsement, service or supervision conditions. Completing this
lecture alone does not satisfy those conditions.

14
00:02:30,864 --> 00:02:42,000
Current D three prohibits PCP cannulation under age twelve by any
method. IO has further restrictions that we will separate from the
broader manufacturer instructions.

15
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:53,880
Use the school model for practice until the actual required education
and authorization apply. Do not generalize a hospital nursing
description or a vendor age range into local permission.

16
00:02:53,880 --> 00:03:05,712
Confirm competence with the actual device and the relevant patient
group. If uncertain, maintain support, seek the required guidance and
choose an authorized alternative.

17
00:03:05,712 --> 00:03:16,344
An IV puts the selected solution into the vascular system without
tissue absorption. What happens afterward depends on its composition
and the patient's physiology.

18
00:03:16,344 --> 00:03:27,096
Water and dissolved particles distribute across compartments
differently. A volume entering the vein is not necessarily the same
amount remaining in plasma over time.

19
00:03:27,096 --> 00:03:38,328
Blood pressure is one part of perfusion assessment, not a complete
measure of tissue flow. A small pressure rise with worsening breathing
can represent harm rather than success.

20
00:03:38,328 --> 00:03:51,216
State an expected physiological endpoint and watch for both benefit
and overload. Later reading develops hydrostatic and osmotic
mechanisms rather than using a simple fill-the-tank slogan.

21
00:03:51,216 --> 00:04:03,168
Before the attempt, assess the reason for access and the current
primary priorities. Identify relevant losses, actual intake, medicines
and previous response where available.

22
00:04:03,168 --> 00:04:13,512
Record useful baseline observations and symptoms, including
respiratory status and perfusion. They give meaning to the response
after fluid or medicine.

23
00:04:13,512 --> 00:04:25,272
Ask about relevant allergy, difficult access, dialysis access, local
injury and prior problems. Explain the proposed route and obtain the
appropriate consent.

24
00:04:25,272 --> 00:04:35,784
Decide who maintains patient support while another person prepares.
Record a reassessment trigger and an escalation plan before repeated
attempts consume attention.

25
00:04:35,784 --> 00:04:47,784
Inspect and palpate before committing to a site. A useful vein is not
just blue or visible; its course, depth and condition must suit the
intended catheter and therapy.

26
00:04:47,784 --> 00:04:58,992
Assess a soft, compressible, nonpulsatile segment using the trained
method. An artery, tendon or scar can be misidentified if you depend
only on appearance.

27
00:04:58,992 --> 00:05:09,792
Look for a segment with enough usable course and avoid injured,
infected or obviously compromised tissue. Patient comfort and limb
function also matter.

28
00:05:09,792 --> 00:05:20,832
When volume depletion makes veins difficult, do not repeatedly probe
the same area. Reassess the need, equipment, help and actual
escalation options.

29
00:05:20,832 --> 00:05:31,872
Starting distally can preserve future options when the vein and
therapy are suitable. It is not an absolute command to use a poor hand
vein for every urgent treatment.

30
00:05:31,872 --> 00:05:44,064
Catheter length, vein size, expected movement and the required therapy
affect selection. A site at a flexion point may obstruct or become
uncomfortable during transport.

31
00:05:44,064 --> 00:05:57,096
Protect an arteriovenous fistula or graft and assess local infection,
trauma, edema and relevant prior procedures. Resolve uncertainty
through the actual policy and clinical plan.

32
00:05:57,096 --> 00:06:08,064
Specific pathways can add considerations, such as preserving access
for an anticipated coronary procedure. Do not let an ideal-site search
create a conveyance delay.

33
00:06:08,064 --> 00:06:18,648
Choose a suitable catheter for the vein, patient and therapy. A larger
bore can support greater flow but may increase local trauma if poorly
matched.

34
00:06:18,648 --> 00:06:29,664
Flow depends on more than gauge: length, connectors, tubing, pressure
and resistance all matter. Do not assume a colour alone proves
capacity or suitability.

35
00:06:29,664 --> 00:06:39,360
A steel collection needle and a flexible over-the-needle catheter
leave different things in the patient. Identify exactly what you are
handling before beginning.

36
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:50,880
Blood-control and needle-safety mechanisms are product-specific.
Practise the actual model; do not blend a previous device's hand
motions into an unfamiliar catheter.

37
00:06:50,880 --> 00:07:00,840
Gather the full setup and inspect packaging, expiry and integrity. Put
the sharps container within the appropriate safe working position
before beginning.

38
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:12,864
Prepare the selected catheter, site-cleansing material and venous
tourniquet. Prepare a primed compatible connector and the prescribed
solution or flush using the actual equipment.

39
00:07:12,864 --> 00:07:22,704
Have the dressing and securement ready so a successful catheter is not
left unsupported while someone searches for tape. Keep critical parts
protected.

40
00:07:22,704 --> 00:07:34,104
Make a plan for a failed attempt and for patient deterioration.
Preparation is a safety step that reduces interruption, contamination
and uncontrolled sharps handling.

41
00:07:34,104 --> 00:07:45,792
Aseptic continuity is not one alcohol wipe at the beginning. The skin,
catheter, extension and access surfaces must remain appropriately
protected throughout the sequence.

42
00:07:45,792 --> 00:07:56,280
Apply the approved antiseptic with the specified contact and drying
time. Re-palpating prepared skin with a contaminated finger breaks the
intended barrier.

43
00:07:56,280 --> 00:08:08,208
Protect critical parts when opening, connecting and securing. Gloves
do not make a touched sterile surface clean, and a connector called
needleless still needs correct aseptic access.

44
00:08:08,208 --> 00:08:19,872
If contamination occurs, identify it and restore the barrier using the
actual trained method. Do not hide a break because the time target or
a partner's expectation feels urgent.

45
00:08:19,872 --> 00:08:32,568
In supervised model practice, stabilize the limb and site and follow
the device's insertion method. The exact angle and advancement are not
universal across products and tissue depths.

46
00:08:32,568 --> 00:08:43,920
Blood return can show needle entry without proving that the flexible
catheter tip is fully within the vein. Some devices have separate
needle and catheter flashback cues.

47
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:55,776
The manufacturer example in the source notes illustrates that
distinction; it is not evidence the school uses that catheter. Ask the
instructor to demonstrate the actual product's transitions.

48
00:08:55,776 --> 00:09:08,208
Maintain control and do not fish blindly. If the expected cue is
absent or the patient has concerning pain, stop and reassess rather
than forcing a successful-looking result.

49
00:09:08,208 --> 00:09:21,624
Once appropriate entry is confirmed, advance the flexible catheter
using the actual trained device method. Do not assume every catheter
uses the same flashback chamber or withdrawal sequence.

50
00:09:21,624 --> 00:09:30,120
Never reinsert a withdrawn needle into the catheter. It can damage or
shear the catheter and create an embolic complication.

51
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:42,192
Release the venous tourniquet as required, stabilize the catheter and
remove the needle using its intended safety mechanism. Avoid
uncontrolled bending or a loose exposed sharp.

52
00:09:42,192 --> 00:09:52,992
Connect promptly with protected compatible parts and secure the route.
Product-specific blood control does not excuse leaving a hub open or
touching critical surfaces.

53
00:09:52,992 --> 00:10:05,448
If cannulation fails, use the actual safe removal sequence and manage
the site. Inspect the device and control bleeding, especially when the
patient has an increased bleeding risk.

54
00:10:05,448 --> 00:10:14,376
Do not reuse a catheter or needle, repeatedly probe damaged tissue or
reinsert a steel needle into a partly withdrawn plastic catheter.

55
00:10:14,376 --> 00:10:25,416
Record attempts according to the actual policy and include colleagues'
attempts in collective limits when specified. An unsuccessful stick is
not erased by later success.

56
00:10:25,416 --> 00:10:37,176
Reassess the current need and conveyance priorities. Two missed IVs do
not independently authorize IO in a conscious dehydrated patient; its
separate conditions must be met.

57
00:10:37,176 --> 00:10:46,776
Before using the route, inspect the site and ask about discomfort if
possible. Trace the connection all the way from the intended solution
to the patient.

58
00:10:46,776 --> 00:10:58,920
Assess according to the actual catheter/therapy method. Neither a
previous flashback nor a momentary easy flush proves that future
delivery will remain intravascular.

59
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:09,384
Resistance, new pain, swelling or leakage is not a reason to use
greater force. Stop and evaluate the cause and the patient before
proceeding.

60
00:11:09,384 --> 00:11:18,576
Recheck after repositioning and during delivery. A route that was
satisfactory before lifting the patient can be displaced or kinked
afterward.

61
00:11:18,576 --> 00:11:29,160
Use the intended securement to reduce motion and catheter migration.
Stabilize the extension so a cable tug does not pull directly on the
insertion site.

62
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:39,048
Keep the site assessable with the approved dressing. A thick wrap that
hides tissue swelling can delay recognition of infiltration or
extravasation.

63
00:11:39,048 --> 00:11:48,624
Do not constrict the limb or obscure distal circulation. Check comfort
and function, and label the required device and insertion details.

64
00:11:48,624 --> 00:12:00,384
After every transfer, reassess the patient, site and complete route
rather than only looking at the fluid bag. Securement success is
demonstrated by continued safe function.

65
00:12:00,384 --> 00:12:11,520
This original arithmetic example uses a simulation order of one
hundred millilitres over twenty minutes. That equals five millilitres
per minute or three hundred per hour.

66
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:23,136
With a training set explicitly labelled fifteen drops per millilitre,
multiply five by fifteen to obtain seventy-five drops per minute. A
different set changes that answer.

67
00:12:23,136 --> 00:12:35,400
Read the actual drop factor and verify the device programming or
gravity delivery. No universal drop volume exists, and setting a rate
does not prove that the intended amount reached the vein.

68
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:45,744
This is not a clinical fluid prescription. In care, actual indication,
limits, delivered amount, response and local instructions govern the
plan.

69
00:12:45,744 --> 00:13:16,008
The school also assigns a BD single-port closed-catheter
demonstration. Its actual product name is Nexiva. The bilingual guide
now follows the complete available narrative, separating needle entry,
catheter advancement, safe needle withdrawal and the later clamp-and-
connection sequence. Initial blood flash does not by itself prove a
correctly positioned, patent catheter. Do not apply one model’s grip,
push-tab or vent-plug instructions to a different device.

70
00:13:16,008 --> 00:13:46,008
Use the video with the existing supervised vascular-access sequence:
consent, the actual indication, aseptic preparation, safe handling,
securement and verification of delivery and response. The linked
historical six-rights article adds concentration and documentation
checks, but it does not replace the school’s expanded medication
framework. A named device and a completed checklist are not proof that
the patient received the intended treatment safely.

71
00:13:46,008 --> 00:13:56,688
Verify the solution and actual indication rather than using fluids as
an automatic response to every low pressure. Different causes of shock
require different priorities.

72
00:13:56,688 --> 00:14:07,608
Current D three includes fluid reassessment and a consultation
requirement when more than two litres is anticipated. That is not a
target to give two litres to everyone.

73
00:14:07,608 --> 00:14:18,792
Use the actual age, pathway and clinical condition for bolus
decisions. Do not turn an adult or hospital example into an
unrestricted pediatric PCP instruction.

74
00:14:18,792 --> 00:14:31,008
Watch breathing as well as pressure and perfusion. If new respiratory
distress appears, stop automatic escalation, reassess the patient and
obtain the appropriate plan.

75
00:14:31,008 --> 00:14:40,920
Reassess after each delivered intervention, not only after a bag is
empty. Compare the patient with the baseline and the endpoint you
stated.

76
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:52,656
Ask what changed: awareness, symptoms, pulse/pressure, breathing and
local access findings. Then ask whether the expected benefit actually
occurred.

77
00:14:52,656 --> 00:15:02,568
Ask what got worse. A pressure rise accompanied by new crackles or an
increasing oxygen requirement is not an uncomplicated success.

78
00:15:02,568 --> 00:15:11,712
Use the answer to update the plan with the appropriate authority. Do
not keep the original infusion running by habit while deterioration
accumulates.

79
00:15:11,712 --> 00:15:23,544
Local pain, swelling, blanching, leakage or altered distal function
requires prompt assessment. An infusion can continue into tissue even
without a pump alarm.

80
00:15:23,544 --> 00:15:36,072
For suspected extravasation, do not flush. Preserve or remove the
device according to the actual product-specific management plan rather
than automatically applying a generic removal rule.

81
00:15:36,072 --> 00:15:49,368
Systemic breathlessness, collapse or new instability needs immediate
patient assessment and support. Do not assume every problem near an IV
has the same cause or the same positioning solution.

82
00:15:49,368 --> 00:16:01,488
Communicate exact product, concentration, amount, rate, timing and
site findings. Document both the clinical care and the required
incident process truthfully.

83
00:16:01,488 --> 00:16:11,616
An intraosseous device provides access to the medullary vascular
space. It can enable indicated therapy when the actual conditions for
IO are met.

84
00:16:11,616 --> 00:16:22,416
A device brochure may list multiple sites and ages. Local PCP
permission is narrower and must be checked separately from what the
product is technically designed to do.

85
00:16:22,416 --> 00:16:34,152
Insertion and infusion can cause different pain, and malposition can
cause serious tissue injury. A catheter standing in bone is not proof
that its tip or flow is correct.

86
00:16:34,152 --> 00:16:46,032
Do not let access placement impair airway support, high-quality
compressions or defibrillation during arrest. Route creation supports
the care plan rather than becoming its main event.

87
00:16:46,032 --> 00:17:03,432
Current PR twelve requires the relevant PCP scope-expansion education.
Its restricted arrest branch concerns patients twelve or older with
clear hypovolemia and fluids critical to the resuscitation plan, after
the specified peripheral-access failure or unavailability.

88
00:17:03,432 --> 00:17:15,576
The page also permits placement under direct supervision of an on-
scene ACP or higher. A telephone conversation is not automatically the
same thing as that specified supervision.

89
00:17:15,576 --> 00:17:28,104
PCP use is tibial only and limited to two collective attempts per
patient. A failed or dislodged site must not be re-cannulated, and the
stated under-twelve restriction remains.

90
00:17:28,104 --> 00:17:43,272
Therefore, an awake vomiting patient with difficult IV access is not
an independent PCP IO indication merely because two attempts failed.
The school case needs an authorized escalation/supervision decision.

91
00:17:43,272 --> 00:17:55,080
Check the intended limb for trauma, local tissue damage, infection,
relevant surgery and joint replacement. Do not choose a convenient-
looking point without a full site check.

92
00:17:55,080 --> 00:18:05,136
Current PR twelve includes recent same-site placement within forty-
eight hours as a contraindication. Document known prior attempts and
their locations.

93
00:18:05,136 --> 00:18:16,200
If landmarks cannot be identified reliably, the site is not made safe
by estimating a fixed distance on the skin. Obtain the appropriate
alternative plan and help.

94
00:18:16,200 --> 00:18:26,448
Count all collective attempts and preserve circulation and function.
Site screening is part of the clinical safety decision, not a
formality after drilling.

95
00:18:26,448 --> 00:18:35,760
For the adult proximal-tibial manufacturer example, support and extend
the leg and palpate the actual patella and tibial tuberosity.

96
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:49,680
The cited adult guide locates a point about two centimetres medial to
the tuberosity on the flat tibial surface. This is a relational
landmark, not permission to drill any two-centimetre skin estimate.

97
00:18:49,680 --> 00:19:02,448
Confirm the anatomy, absence of contraindications and the actual local
taught site with an instructor on the model. Do not import the
brochure's pediatric or humeral sites into PCP permission.

98
00:19:02,448 --> 00:19:11,568
Landmarking must be checked before insertion. A figure or verbal
description cannot demonstrate that your fingers have located the
correct structure.

99
00:19:11,568 --> 00:19:23,232
Prepare the actual device and suitable needle set, primed extension,
stabilization equipment and therapy plan. Check packaging and the
driver before contacting the patient.

100
00:19:23,232 --> 00:19:37,248
Needle length must account for tissue depth. The cited EZ-IO guide
requires the specified length marking to remain visible at bone
contact before powered insertion; colour or body weight alone is not
enough.

101
00:19:37,248 --> 00:19:47,712
Use the actual trained angle and controlled gentle technique, not
excessive force. Do not invent a depth or keep drilling after the
expected insertion endpoint.

102
00:19:47,712 --> 00:19:59,640
Stabilize the device while removing the driver and stylet as trained,
immediately contain the sharp and use a protected primed connection. A
generic lecture is not a device checkoff.

103
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:12,672
Use the actual placement and patency checks, including aspiration
where specified, site observation and response to the approved test.
No single cue proves every later infusion safe.

104
00:20:12,672 --> 00:20:24,840
Observe for soft-tissue swelling, leakage, unexpected pain and distal
neurovascular change. Suspected malposition or extravasation is a
stop-and-reassess problem.

105
00:20:24,840 --> 00:20:34,728
Flow may require the appropriate trained pressure setup, but greater
pressure must not be used to overcome a route that may be misplaced or
causing tissue injury.

106
00:20:34,728 --> 00:20:43,680
Reconfirm before further administration and after moving the person. A
stable-looking hub is not sufficient evidence of safe delivery.

107
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:54,864
An IO insertion and an IO infusion can hurt differently. A patient who
responds to pain needs an appropriate plan even if they cannot carry
on a normal conversation.

108
00:20:54,864 --> 00:21:09,168
Current PR twelve places conscious-patient lidocaine administration
with ACP or a PCP under direction of an on-scene ACP. Do not infer an
independent PCP anesthetic permission from a classroom case.

109
00:21:09,168 --> 00:21:22,680
Verify the actual medicine formulation and order, relevant
contraindications, extension volume and delivery timing. A dose
sitting in the extension has not necessarily reached the medullary
space.

110
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:34,152
Continue monitoring and communicate inadequate pain relief or
complications. Do not improvise an extra dose or copy a manufacturer's
optional regimen into a local order.

111
00:21:34,152 --> 00:21:47,640
The saved school Wednesday case says zero point five milligrams per
kilogram over sixty seconds. That wording is retained as a source
discrepancy, not silently rewritten as if the school said something
else.

112
00:21:47,640 --> 00:22:01,344
Current PR twelve states forty milligrams for the conscious adult,
slowly over one hundred and twenty seconds, then a sixty-second dwell
before the subsequent specified flush, under its stated clinical
authority.

113
00:22:01,344 --> 00:22:12,624
Its child weight-based statement does not erase the PCP under-twelve
cannulation prohibition. Adult versus child wording and on-scene
supervision remain separate requirements.

114
00:22:12,624 --> 00:22:25,248
For a station, explicitly identify the discrepancy and ask the
instructor for the current approved sequence. Do not invent a hybrid
dose, sixty-second rate or extra-dose schedule.

115
00:22:25,248 --> 00:22:36,456
Protect the catheter during movement and keep the site/limb accessible
to reassessment. A displacement can change delivery from medullary
circulation to soft tissue.

116
00:22:36,456 --> 00:22:49,224
Assess pain, swelling, leakage and distal function. Increasing tissue
tension or neurovascular concern requires urgent appropriate action,
not a tighter wrap and more pressure.

117
00:22:49,224 --> 00:23:00,576
Record the device/site, collective attempts, time, exact
medicines/fluids and response. Report partial delivery or a failed
route truthfully.

118
00:23:00,576 --> 00:23:11,880
Use the actual clinical plan for ongoing access and removal. A
manufacturer's maximum dwell time is not a reason to retain an
unnecessary or compromised route.

119
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:23,544
This original scenario follows the school learning objective without
copying its patient. A forty-three-year-old has prolonged vomiting,
poor intake, weakness and low blood pressure.

120
00:23:23,544 --> 00:23:35,688
Assess immediate threats and ask what else could this be. Volume loss
may be important, but other causes, ongoing losses, medicines and the
actual response must be considered.

121
00:23:35,688 --> 00:23:48,672
If indicated access is difficult, improve site/device planning, seek
help and reassess priorities. Two failures alone do not meet the
restricted awake-patient PCP IO pathway.

122
00:23:48,672 --> 00:24:00,288
Under an authorized plan, evaluate every intervention against the
expected benefit and new harm. Report the patient and access status,
not just that you eventually got a line.

123
00:24:00,288 --> 00:24:11,160
Explain the clinical indication and the ongoing patient concern. A
receiving clinician needs to know why the route was established and
whether that reason still applies.

124
00:24:11,160 --> 00:24:24,504
State the actual site/device, insertion time, attempts and any
difficulty or modification. For IO, communicate the actual supervision
and pain-management plan as relevant.

125
00:24:24,504 --> 00:24:36,384
Report exact products, concentrations, amounts and rates, including
partial administration and relevant tubing residual. A prepared amount
is not always the delivered amount.

126
00:24:36,384 --> 00:24:47,928
Close with response, complications, current patency and the next care
plan. Complete the clinical record and required incident reporting
without disguising uncertainty.

127
00:24:47,928 --> 00:25:41,616
Compare the actual reference entries rather than silently harmonizing
them. This PHARM copy lists serum potassium 3.6-5.4 mEq/L, sodium
135-145 mEq/L, total calcium 8.8-10.4 mg/dL, magnesium 1.46-2.68 mEq/L
and chloride 98-106 mEq/L. The separately assigned FON table uses some
different intervals. The PHARM phosphate numbers labelled mEq/L remain
a unit problem, not a validated equivalent table. For a patient,
retain the actual laboratory analyte, specimen, unit and interval;
neither textbook defines every clinical action threshold.

128
00:25:41,616 --> 00:26:26,016
The sodium-potassium pump maintains unequal compartments by using ATP
to move three sodium ions out and two potassium ions in per cycle. It
is not a measurement of the serum potassium or a guarantee of a normal
ECG. In medication history, potassium supplements and potassium-
containing salt substitutes can interact importantly with impaired
renal excretion, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium-sparing diuretics.
Distinguish a prescribed replacement plan from unrestricted
supplementation. The source peripheral potassium concentration/rate
example is not a universal safe limit and does not override the
prohibition on rapid IV potassium push.

129
00:26:26,016 --> 00:27:10,392
Absorption interactions are part of the assigned mineral teaching.
Oral calcium can reduce absorption of levothyroxine and certain
tetracycline/quinolone antibiotics; calcium/dairy and oral iron also
need product-specific scheduling. The source separately flags
magnesium with iron, folic-acid or fibre supplements. Identify the
actual formulations and prescribed spacing rather than combining all
products together or imposing one interval on every medicine. Ask
about nonprescription supplements and antacids. Education about diet
or drinking water must remain individualized, especially with renal or
heart disease and a fluid restriction.

130
00:27:10,392 --> 00:27:53,592
Connect the PN adverse-effect list to surveillance: catheter infection
or thrombosis, fluid overload, glucose disturbances, liver
complications and refeeding-related electrolyte shifts are different
problems. A concentrated PN product is not an ordinary bag to restart
or replace at a guessed rate; a PICC may enter peripherally but have a
central tip. Urine sugar alone does not monitor glucose safety, and a
printed twelve-hour intake/output example is not the reassessment
interval for an acutely deteriorating patient. Report the actual
product, access, amount/rate, interruption and new findings to the
responsible team.

131
00:27:53,592 --> 00:28:33,000
The first assigned cardiac page carries over preload, afterload and
contractility before 16.3. Correct its venous-direction shortcut:
venoconstriction can mobilize venous blood toward the heart, whereas
venodilation can promote pooling; less venoconstriction does not
automatically increase preload. Filling pressure and filling volume
depend on compliance, and more filling does not always increase useful
output in a failing ventricle. Inotropy can be positive or negative.
Use these distinctions to explain why pressure improvement alone does
not prove a successful fluid intervention.

132
00:28:33,000 --> 00:29:14,688
Read each ECG lead as its own potential-difference view, not a
numerical sum of twelve lead traces. Depolarization moving toward a
lead positive pole generally produces an upward deflection; vector
direction and repolarization conventions matter. P, QRS and T relate
to atrial depolarization, ventricular depolarization and ventricular
repolarization, not measured flow. The assigned final page includes
the Chapter 16 summary and key terms, whose artery/vein wording still
needs the direction-versus-oxygen correction. A named normal rhythm
does not prove normal perfusion or exclude ischemia.

133
00:29:14,688 --> 00:30:01,800
The literal last page, printed 140, already starts 5.4: its objectives
introduce vitamins/minerals, chelation, complementary therapies,
interactions and patient education; the visible body distinguishes
water-soluble from fat-soluble vitamins. Water-soluble vitamins
generally need ongoing intake and renal handling matters, whereas fat-
soluble vitamins can accumulate and cause toxicity. Do not turn this
into a rule that every water-soluble vitamin has no storage: vitamin
B12 has substantial body stores. Malabsorption, losses and increased
requirements can matter. The following detailed vitamin table and
chelator sections are not inside this assigned page span.

134
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:45,576
The literal first page also completes the preceding respiratory-
specimen topic. Collection technique, specimen quality, contamination,
timing, prior medicines and transport/storage can change sputum or
nasal/throat results. Older adults may have a weaker cough, thicker
secretions or difficulty understanding instructions; adapt
communication and assistance without forcing an unsafe collection. A
swab must match the intended test. A poorly collected or late specimen
can mislead even when the laboratory method works correctly. This
carryover is reading context, not an additional assessed Week 3 sputum
station.

135
00:30:45,576 --> 00:31:28,080
A vacuum collection tube draws blood because its internal pressure is
lower; the holder supports the compatible collection system. The
source photograph uses lavender for a CBC and red for serum testing,
but the actual additive, test, fill and mixing instructions control
selection, not colour alone. Preserve patient identification at the
bedside and do not transfer blood between tubes to repair a wrong
collection. Its statement about whole blood being a fixed fraction of
extracellular fluid also needs caution: plasma is extracellular, while
red-cell water is intracellular; whole blood and plasma are not the
same compartment.

136
00:31:28,080 --> 00:32:13,680
Read the CVAD diagrams as separate channels, not as a guarantee of
compatible medicines. Its illustrated triple-lumen table uses
proximal/middle 18-gauge and distal 16-gauge channels, with differing
assigned uses; another catheter can have a different layout. The
sampling table includes examples of 10-mL syringes, 10-20 mL saline
flushes and 2-25 mL discarded blood. These depend on the device, dead
space, test, patient and approved method, not a universal sequence to
apply to a child or unfamiliar line. Protect critical infusions, use
approved aseptic access and avoid unnecessary blood loss.

137
00:32:13,680 --> 00:32:56,496
For the capillary-site drawing, distinguish an adult fingertip example
from infant heel sampling. The source infant example uses the medial
or lateral plantar heel surface and avoids the centre and posterior
curve. Use the actual age-appropriate, depth-limited device and
authorized procedure; an illustrated site is not approval for every
glucose meter. Excessive squeezing can dilute the sample with tissue
fluid or cause hemolysis. Follow actual cleaning/drying and first-drop
instructions for the test, record the site/time/result and respond to
a critically ill patient without waiting through an unnecessary
sampling ritual.

138
00:32:56,496 --> 00:33:44,496
The assigned IV-drug examples span antibiotics, analgesics, emergency
medicines, chemotherapy and immunoglobulin. Examples include
vancomycin, ketorolac, fentanyl, naloxone and seizure medicines; they
illustrate different indications and monitoring, not a new PCP drug
list. Alteplase is a tissue plasminogen activator, not a second
unrelated drug beside tPA. IVIG is an antibody product, not an
antibiotic. Bioavailability is the fraction reaching systemic
circulation, not the speed of effect, and IV delivery does not make
every medicine safe as a rapid push. Identify an existing infusion by
actual name, concentration, route and order.

139
00:33:44,496 --> 00:34:33,000
Read the hardware labels, not a memorized appearance. Lower gauge
numbers mean larger internal diameter for otherwise comparable
catheters; the source wording later reverses larger and smaller gauge
descriptions. Its macrodrip examples are 10, 15 or 20 gtt/mL and
microdrip 60 gtt/mL; use the package value. In the source
primary/secondary arrangement, bag height and a suitable backcheck
valve help select flow, but compatibility, residual tubing volume and
the actual flush plan determine delivery. A smart or syringe pump
still needs the correct product, syringe, settings and patient/site
checks; it is not a universal interaction detector.

140
00:34:33,000 --> 00:35:16,968
Secure the catheter without hiding the assessment site or creating
constriction. The source injury story illustrates why obscured
swelling and delayed recognition can cause lasting harm. Keep the
skin, insertion site and distal function inspectable according to the
actual dressing/device plan, and respond to pain or new swelling
rather than trusting that fluid is still running. The distal-first
principle preserves options; its phrase about leaking into the
intravascular space is misleading when describing infiltration into
surrounding tissue. A vein map or near-infrared locator aids location,
not proof of successful intravascular placement or freedom from
nerve/artery injury.

141
00:35:16,968 --> 00:35:59,400
Original linked encounter: an adult receiving a home infusion reports
new arm pain and swelling, and a critical laboratory result was drawn
from the infusing side. Assess and support the patient, stop the
implicated delivery appropriately, protect the site and obtain the
product/device-specific care plan. The sample may be contaminated or
hemolyzed, but that possibility does not prove the dangerous result
false or justify ignoring deterioration. Communicate the source of the
sample, actual infusion and new findings; arrange appropriate
confirmation while immediate care continues. Do not force-flush the
line to make it look patent.

142
00:35:59,400 --> 00:36:44,832
The literal printed 1022 page is a hearing/balance page, not section
18.3. Interpret its tuning-fork drawing carefully: Rinne compares air
with bone conduction; air greater than bone can be normal or
sensorineural loss, whereas bone greater than air supports a
conductive component. Weber at the midline normally remains central,
or may remain central with equal bilateral loss; in a unilateral
pattern it tends toward the conductive-loss ear or away from the
sensorineural-loss ear. The picture label normal on one side is not a
rule that normal hearing lateralizes. Formal audiology may be needed;
this is not a new Week 3 assessed tuning-fork station.

143
00:36:44,832 --> 00:37:27,288
The same page describes balance observation, a Romberg comparison,
facial-nerve findings and the start of validating/documenting
findings. A positive Romberg classically means relative stability with
eyes open but marked loss of stability after closing them; it does not
by itself diagnose an inner-ear disease. Instability with eyes open is
already abnormal, not reassurance because the book calls the
comparison negative. Guard against falling and do not provoke the test
in someone unsafe to stand. Record actual gait/balance and facial
asymmetry with the rest of the neurological assessment rather than
labeling every dizzy patient a benign ear problem.

144
00:37:27,288 --> 00:37:38,232
Without reading, explain the access indication, the patient risk and
the alternative route you considered. State why hospital convenience
or practice alone is not enough.

145
00:37:38,232 --> 00:37:48,768
On the model, lay out the full equipment and describe the protected
parts and actual device transitions. A partner should be able to
identify a contamination break.

146
00:37:48,768 --> 00:37:59,448
Describe the response to a failed attempt, swelling during a flush and
a new breathing problem during fluid. None is solved by blindly
increasing force or rate.

147
00:37:59,448 --> 00:38:11,400
Finish by distinguishing the school IO scenario from current PR twelve
and by using the three post-intervention questions. These are
preparation, not a signed practical qualification.
